          <mxGeometry x="164" y="925" width="482" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="lg-l6" style="endArrow=blockThin;endFill=1;startArrow=blockThin;startFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#0078D4;strokeWidth=2;dashed=1;dashPattern=8 4;" parent="1" edge="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint x="84" y="966" as="sourcePoint" />
            <mxPoint x="152" y="966" as="targetPoint" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="lg-t6" value="&lt;b&gt;VNet peering&lt;/b&gt;, both directions — so one client can reach both gateways" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=10;fontColor=#33415C;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="164" y="953" width="482" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="lg-l7" style="endArrow=blockThin;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#605E5C;strokeWidth=1.5;" parent="1" edge="1">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint x="84" y="994" as="sourcePoint" />
            <mxPoint x="152" y="994" as="targetPoint" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="lg-t7" value="&lt;b&gt;Outbound egress&lt;/b&gt; — how a private-IP container pulls its image through NAT" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=10;fontColor=#33415C;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="164" y="981" width="482" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="rbac-box" value="Identity and least privilege" style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FDFBFF;strokeColor=#5C2D91;strokeWidth=1.5;dashed=1;dashPattern=8 4;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=16;spacingTop=8;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#4B2379;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="60" y="1050" width="600" height="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="rbac-text" value="&lt;b&gt;No keys and no secrets anywhere.&lt;/b&gt; Every ARM call uses ManagedIdentityCredential against the Function App&#39;s system-assigned identity, and even AzureWebJobsStorage is identity-based.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Private AppGW DR Watchdog&lt;/b&gt; — a purpose-built role at subscription scope:&lt;br&gt;• read container groups&lt;br&gt;• read Application Gateways&lt;br&gt;• POST applicationGateways/backendhealth&lt;br&gt;• read Microsoft.Insights metrics (probe host counts)&lt;br&gt;assigned on &lt;b&gt;rg-appgw-dr-eus&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;rg-appgw-dr-cus&lt;/b&gt;. Reader does not cover backendhealth because it is a POST action, and Network Contributor would grant far too much.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Private DNS Zone Contributor&lt;/b&gt; on internal.contoso.com — the identity&#39;s only write permission anywhere. Three storage data-plane roles make the keyless AzureWebJobsStorage work." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=10;fontColor=#33415C;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="80" y="1096" width="562" height="200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="steps-box" value="How traffic flows, step by step" style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#F7F9FC;strokeColor=#243A5E;strokeWidth=1.5;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=16;spacingTop=8;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#243A5E;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="700" y="1050" width="820" height="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="steps-text" value="&lt;b&gt;1&lt;/b&gt;   The client resolves &lt;b&gt;app.internal.contoso.com&lt;/b&gt;. It never learns a gateway address, which is what makes the failover transparent.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;2&lt;/b&gt;   The private A record answers &lt;b&gt;10.1.1.10&lt;/b&gt;, the East US gateway&#39;s private frontend. TTL is 30 seconds, matching the watchdog&#39;s cadence.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;3&lt;/b&gt;   The gateway forwards HTTP :80 to &lt;b&gt;aci-eus-dr&lt;/b&gt; and probes it on &lt;b&gt;/&lt;/b&gt; with an explicit 127.0.0.1 host header, since the backend pool holds bare IPs with no FQDN to derive one from.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;4&lt;/b&gt;   Every 30 seconds the watchdog reads container state, then custom probe health (&lt;b&gt;backendhealth&lt;/b&gt;, or host-count metrics if that call returns no servers). &lt;b&gt;alert-primary-probe-down&lt;/b&gt; emails the action group and calls the same code immediately.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style=&#39;color:#107C10&#39;&gt;5&lt;/b&gt;   If East US is not up &lt;i&gt;right now&lt;/i&gt;, the watchdog PUTs the A record to &lt;b&gt;10.2.1.10&lt;/b&gt;. It reads the record first, so a steady state performs no writes at all.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b style=&#39;color:#D83B01&#39;&gt;6&lt;/b&gt;   Clients pick up the new address within one TTL and Central US serves. Failback happens only once &lt;b&gt;aci-eus-dr&lt;/b&gt; is Running and the primary probe is up — never on a lagging HealthyHostCount sample alone." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=10;fontColor=#33415C;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="720" y="1098" width="782" height="196" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="footer" value="Derived from terraform/README.md, FILES.md, and the Terraform configuration. Names, address prefixes, and thresholds are the module defaults in variables.tf." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=9;fontColor=#8A8886;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1580" y="1296" width="500" height="40" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
